/usr/bin/concat from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for concat, so this is the disassembly, and concat.c is that
disassembly read back into C.

    disas -f concat.ctl concat.dist

concat.dist is the binary off inode 59 of disks/dist/1012-8_dist_2.IMD, 5181
bytes, mode 0711, dated 7 August 1982.  It has no header - see
../newuser/README - and uses Whitesmith's runtime, c.ent at 1263 and c.ents at
1116.  It is the program of that name in Kernighan and Plauger's Software Tools.

Its own code is main at 010e, copy at 01bc and the error routine at 01fe, with
": can't open" at 01f0.  main attaches the standard output with finit, and for
each argument opens it with the library's fopen (0227), calls copy, and closes
it with fclose (07a1).  A file that will not open is "name: can't open" on the
standard error, and exit.  copy is a loop:

    do
        n = getl(fp, buf, 512)       042a
    while (putl(stdout, buf, n))     058c

THE ORIGINAL COPIES ONE LINE OF EACH FILE, and that is not a thing the manual
says.  getl reads up to a newline or 512 characters.  putl is the library's
block write, and it returns the count it was given - except when the stream is
the standard output and the block ends in a newline, when it flushes and
returns what the flush returned, which is 0.  The loop's test is the return of
putl, so the first block that ends in a newline ends the loop.  A file of
"a", newline, "b", newline writes "a" and a newline, and a first line of 600
characters is written whole - the first 512 do not end in a newline and the
loop goes on - and then no more.  It is the same whether the output is a file
or a terminal.

That is the original's, and it is so on the 1982 library as much as on this
one: the tests were run in usersim with a terminal and with a file as the
output and came out the same.  concat.c does what the manual says and copies
all of every file.  It does not keep the loss, and the one behaviour that
differs from the original's is this one.

The standard error, the standard output of a file of one line, an empty file,
no files, and a file that will not open are as the original's - no files writes
nothing and does not read the standard input; and what was written before a
file that would not open is written.  The exit status was 0 for the file that
would not open and for a good run whatever was in BC when the argument list ran
out; concat.c exits 1 and 0.

Checked in usersim against the original, byte for byte, standard error and all,
on single-line files, empty files, files with no newline, no files, a missing
file in the middle of a list, and the same file twice.  The files of more than
a line were compared by hand, and the original's output of them is the first
line.
